optimizations  algorithms and schedulers present and  in radio  and it allows you to  specify the resource requirements for  your models  so here is a quick demo  of how tuna scaler can be used we are  using a driver safety prediction data  set which has around 600 000 rows  and 50 columns  it uses a cluster that has been started  before through ray cluster launcher  which composes five nodes each with  eight cpus  and we are using a jupiter notebook that  runs on the head node  so  first  we will connect  to our ray cluster  by using gray in it  and we can see how many cpu resources we  have  this is the scikit-learn code randomized  search cv but we can very easily  turn it  into  tune search cv we replace the uh  sci-pi distributions with tuned  distributions for hyper parameters  and we simply replace  random randomized search cv with two  search cv  and we can choose how many  trials we want to run in parallel and we  can also take advantage of early  stopping as described before  and just like that and we get our result  and we can access the entire history of  optimization  through the analysis object returned by  tune search cv  and we can also  plot the  cumulative maximum score over time  finally we disconnect from the array  cluster  uh thank you this concludes my talk uh  let's keep in touch uh here are some  links to our to raid website our  discussion forum and our slack  and to  the github page for tune sklearn  i am happy to take any questions you  might have now  thank you so much anthony  uh here we have a question from  um i don't know if i'm going to  pronounce your name correctly and at all  so anatol asks do you provide classical  parameter grids for most popular  algorithms  we  do not do that however  there is a project that is  being conducted right now that  eventually aims to allow  for us to suggest  which combination of  search algorithms and parameter grids  would be most suitable for a given task  however this will probably take some  time before it is complete that being  said there are many  auto email libraries that are based on  raytune or have support for retune like  ludwig  which provide  pre-made parameter grids  okay thank you so much  um i think we have another question here  oh it says uh how does rey work together  with ml flow  i see certain overlap but not fully  yes but not fully  right so  ray by itself only provides  apis and primitives for other  libraries to be built upon however  raytune  has an integration of ml flow a callback  that reports the results to ml flow so  you can  maintain a unified history of the  optimization of mlflow we also support  weights and biases we also support  tensorboard and it is possible for you  to write your own callbacks for any  bookkeeping solution you may require  okay um i hope video uh got his uh his  or her answer  um we have another question from v  we asked uh she's uh v says i use  darkerized trainers and v and dvc  metrics how hot do you think will be  integrating to ray  um  that's a very good question uh i would  have to  uh  i would have to probably answer that  offline once i know more about your use  case so let's connect on slack if  possible  okay we i hope you heard that uh we'll  get your uh answer on slag  and uh nick asks uh if a single machine  with multiple cars or gp gpus is  powerful enough does ray offer any  advantage  or it is mainly for setting where you  need it faster  so  uh radeon provides a unified api for  various different search algorithms  which allows you to easily  see  which one is the best for your use case  and at least compared to scikit-learn it  allows you to specify resource  requirements for each trial  with a huge degree of flexibility  as as far as i understand scikit-learn  doesn't really support  gpu gpu training however with raytune it  is possible for you to define how many  gpus each trial should use and  that will ensure that the trials  run without any slowdowns or  interruptions  okay if